WHY THERE IS AN ENABLE LIST, AND WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS
------------------------------------------------------------------
This package is light (3.8 MB), so carrying all 162 skills costs nothing on disk. But a skill does not cost disk space,
it costs CONTEXT space: the descriptions of 162 skills add up to ≈ 113 KB ≈ 29 thousand tokens, and that is what the
model rereads on every turn, for every user, on every lane.

And most of them have nothing to do with what Tracy does: 24 image-generation skills, 12 animation skills, 8 slide
skills, 8 video skills, 7 Figma skills — while a Tracy customer has a WordPress or Joomla website that needs
to look better.

`tracy-enabled.txt` is the 20 skills related to web, brand and interface design. The image copies only the ones
named there into the folder that `skill-filesystem` scans, so the other 142 skills sit idle in the
package: readable, reviewable, enabled later with one line — but eating no one's context.

Measured 05/09/2026: of the 52 skills enabled at first, 33 were only stubs — `SKILL.md` opens with "This catalogue
entry advertises the skill…" and tells the agent to install the upstream version into its own skill folder, something
no one can do in a customer lane. They spent the description budget every turn without carrying any
method, so they were dropped; `design-brief` was enabled in addition because it is the only skill that both has real content and
knows about the `design-systems` folder. Machine check: every name in `tracy-enabled.txt` must NOT
match `grep "This catalogue entry advertises"`.

Changing the list means changing one file and building a new image. No one can change it quietly on a server, and its
history is the git history.
